### ownership.hk — memory safety without lifetime annotations.

Halka has no garbage collector and no lifetime syntax. Every heap value has one
owner; borrows are lexical and cannot escape the block that made them. That is
the whole model, and it is checked at compile time.

The commented-out lines are the ones the compiler rejects, with the error it
gives. Uncomment any of them to see it.

Full design: spec/MEMORY-MODEL.md
###

# --- scalars copy, so there is nothing to think about -----------------------

let count: 5
let copy: count
say "scalars copy freely: {count} and {copy}"

# A struct of plain scalars copies too (M1.1) — no clone noise.
Point:
    x: int,
    y: int

let origin: Point(2, 3)
let also: origin
say "a plain struct copies: {origin.x} and {also.y}"

# --- heap values have exactly one owner (M1) --------------------------------

let names: ["alice", "bob"]

# let stolen: names
# say len(names)
#   error[E0504]: `names` was moved and can no longer be used
#      | let stolen: names
#      |             ----- moved here

# --- parameters borrow, so ordinary code never thinks about this (M2.2) -----

describe(items),
    give "a list of {len(items)}"

say describe(names)
say describe(names)
say "still ours: {len(names)}"

# --- ownership is inferred, never annotated (M2.2) --------------------------
# `remember` keeps its argument, so the compiler makes that parameter owning
# and every call site moves. Nothing had to be written down for it.

Registry:
    entries: list(string)

remember(r, entry),
    r.entries.push(entry)

let registry: Registry([])
let label: "first"
remember(registry, label)

# say label
#   error[E0504]: `label` was moved and can no longer be used
#      | remember(registry, label)
#      |                    ----- moved here, because `remember` keeps its argument

say "registry holds {len(registry.entries)}"

# --- borrows are lexical and cannot escape (M2, M2.3) -----------------------

total_of(numbers),
    let sum: 0,
    for n in numbers,
        sum: sum + n,
    give sum

let readings: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]
say "sum through a borrow: {total_of(borrow readings)}"
say "and we still have it: {len(readings)}"

# first_of(items),
#     give borrow items[0]
#   error[E0510]: a borrow cannot be returned — it would outlive its block
#     help: return the value itself, an index into it, or a `shared(T)`

# --- one exclusive borrow, or many shared ones (M2.1) -----------------------
# A named borrow lives to the end of its block, so these two share one.

if true,
    let shared_a: borrow readings,
    let shared_b: borrow readings,
    say "two shared borrows are fine: {len(readings)}"
    # let writer: borrow mut readings
    #   error[E0511]: `readings` cannot be borrowed mutably while it is borrowed

# Both ended with that block, so an exclusive borrow is available now.
if true,
    let writer: borrow mut readings,
    say "and now an exclusive one, because the shared ones ended"

# This is why the model stays small: a borrow's life is a block you can see,
# so the compiler never has to relate two lifetimes, or name either one.

# --- the same rule prevents data races (M5) ---------------------------------

work(data): int,
    give len(data)

# let t: start work(borrow readings)
#   error[E0512]: a task cannot capture a borrow — it may outlive the value
#     help: pass the value itself, or a `shared(T)` if several owners are needed

say "done — no collector, no pauses, no lifetime annotations anywhere"
